Build Specification — App 15: Journal Submission Checker
Completeness status: COMPLETE. All sections delivered in one pass. No truncation. Formatted for copy-paste into another conversation window. No tables, no boxes.

What It Is
A web service that checks a submission against the two reporting standards. It accepts a manuscript plus its STAG annotations and its Null-Model specification, checks them against the reporting standard in each paper, and returns a checklist. It is for journals, preprint servers, and repositories.

Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Accept a manuscript submission.

Accept STAG annotation files.

Accept a Null-Model Protocol pipeline specification.

Check the submission against the STAG reporting standard (Artifact 1, Section 14.1).

Check the submission against the Null-Model Protocol reporting standard (Section 12 of the Null-Model Protocol paper).

Return a checklist report with pass, fail, or missing per item.

Report the reason for each fail or missing item.

Distinguish between items that are required and items that are required-when-applicable.

Distinguish between the STAG reporting standard and the Null-Model Protocol reporting standard.

Detect whether the manuscript uses STAG annotations, Null-Model Protocol specifications, or both.

Detect whether the STAG annotations use the correct schema version.

Detect whether the Null-Model Protocol specification uses the correct schema version.

Detect whether the STAG annotations contain a STAG-Discovered label with its anchoring status alongside.

Detect whether the Null-Model Protocol specification contains the required pre-specification items.

Detect whether the manuscript's reported numbers match the numbers in the annotations and specifications.

Return a machine-readable report (JSON) and a human-readable report (text).

Support batch checking of multiple submissions.

Support a web interface and a command-line interface.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic: same inputs produce same outputs.

No network dependency for the core validation logic (though the service itself is web-based).

Fast: check a typical submission in under ten seconds.

Versioned: the checker records the STAG schema version, the Null-Model Protocol schema version, and the checker version.

Reproducible: the checker records all inputs and the outputs.

Input Specification
Input 1. Manuscript. Required. A document in PDF, LaTeX, or plain text format.

Input 2. STAG annotation files. Optional. One or more STAG annotation records in JSON format.

Input 3. Null-Model Protocol pipeline specification. Optional. A pipeline specification in JSON format.

Input 4. Null-Model Protocol statistical gap results. Optional. One or more statistical gap results in JSON format.

Input 5. STAG schema version. Optional. Defaults to the latest supported version.

Input 6. Null-Model Protocol schema version. Optional. Defaults to the latest supported version.

Input 7. Check mode. Optional. Values: full (default), stag_only, null_model_only, numbers_only.

Input 8. Output format. Optional. Values: json (default), text, both.

Input 9. Submission identifier. Optional. A string identifying the submission.

Output Specification
Output 1. Checklist report. JSON or text. For each item in the STAG reporting standard and the Null-Model Protocol reporting standard, the status: pass, fail, missing, or not_applicable.

Output 2. Per-item reason. Text. For each fail or missing item, the reason.

Output 3. Summary report. Text. The number of items passed, the number of items failed, the number of items missing, and the number of items not applicable.

Output 4. Cross-check report. JSON. For each reported number in the manuscript, whether it matches the corresponding number in the annotation or specification.

Output 5. Detection report. JSON. Whether the manuscript uses STAG annotations, Null-Model Protocol specifications, or both.

Output 6. Reproducibility record. JSON. The STAG schema version, Null-Model Protocol schema version, checker version, submission identifier, and timestamp.

Module-by-Module Specification
Module 1: Manuscript Loader
Reads the manuscript. Supports PDF, LaTeX, and plain text. Extracts the text for analysis. Extracts any structured metadata if present.

Module 2: STAG Annotation Loader
Reads the STAG annotation files. Validates each against the STAG JSON Schema (Artifact 2, Appendix B.1) for the selected version. Rejects malformed records with an error.

Module 3: Null-Model Protocol Loader
Reads the pipeline specification and the statistical gap results. Validates each against the Null-Model Protocol schema. Rejects malformed specifications with an error.

Module 4: Detection Engine
Detects whether the manuscript uses STAG annotations, Null-Model Protocol specifications, or both. Detection is based on:

The presence of STAG annotation files in the submission.

The presence of Null-Model Protocol files in the submission.

The presence of STAG-specific terms in the manuscript text (for example, "STAG-Discovered", "evidential anchoring", "node tuple", "scale-separation record").

The presence of Null-Model Protocol-specific terms in the manuscript text (for example, "empirical null distribution", "exceedance probability", "scan statistic", "empty-W fraction").

The detection report records the detection result and the evidence.

Module 5: STAG Reporting Standard Checker
Checks the submission against the STAG reporting standard (Artifact 1, Section 14.1). The reporting standard has the following items:

Item S1. System and domain; partition identifier and version. Core-tier, required.

Item S2. Candidate effective descriptions. Core-tier, required.

Item S3. Node tuple rule (S, M, G matching rules); M-equivalence rule version; m_equivalence_basis, m_equivalence_witness where claimed; solution-method attribute; domains spanned. Core-tier, required.

Item S4. Node identity decision; component-level match decisions. Core-tier, required.

Item S5. Regime attribute (value; order parameter; control-parameter point; phase-diagram source; domain-specific label and framework mapping; value_bands_if_crossover; regime_band_source; regime_not_determinable flag). Core-tier, required.

Item S6. Node-set agreement. Core-tier, required.

Item S7. Effective-description change (value; criteria fired with evidence; external justification for ΔAPP and ΔEFT). Core-tier, required.

Item S8. Intervention classification (value; entailment_basis; for E1 and E2, cited_structural_result with result_reference, applicability_conditions, and conditions_check; for E3, cited_numerical_result with citation, result_summary, classification_effect, citation_scope; not_determinable where the sources cannot be verified). Core-tier, required.

Item S9. Cross-domain counterfactual dependence (value; source and target domains; intervention_kind; defining_coupling_intervention flag; cross_domain_intervention_available; domain_mismatch; material-change test and result; material_change_test_justification). Core-tier, required.

Item S10. independence_of_material_change_evidence and material_change_test_result_independence. Core-tier, required.

Item S11. Scale-separation record. Extension-tier, required when applicable.

Item S12. Partition; partition sensitivity; identity-splitting audit for merged nodes above STAG-Unclassified. Extension-tier, required when applicable.

Item S13. Display label; evidential_anchoring; unclassified_reason; regime_not_determinable; identity_partition_sensitive; not_determinable flag; agreement_outcome; provisional_flag; threshold_sensitive_flag; evidential_anchoring_status. Derived, required.

Item S14. Threshold provenance. Extension-tier, required when applicable.

Item S15. Characteristic scale. Extension-tier, required when applicable.

Item S16. Reversibility. Extension-tier, required when applicable.

Item S17. Status dimensions. Extension-tier, required when applicable.

Item S18. Statistical gap interface. Extension-tier, required when applicable.

Item S19. Evidence. Core-tier, required.

Item S20. Uncertainty and alternatives. Extension-tier, required when applicable.

Item S21. Theory version. Core-tier, required.

Item S22. Annotator provenance. Core-tier, required.

Item S23. Validity evidence. Core-tier, required.

Item S24. Agreement statistics. Core-tier, required.

Module 6: Null-Model Protocol Reporting Standard Checker
Checks the submission against the Null-Model Protocol reporting standard (Section 12 of the Null-Model Protocol paper). The reporting standard has the following items:

Item N1. Data and population, and measurement definition. Required.

Item N2. Measurement uncertainty, in the form of a kernel, or a justification for neglecting it. Required.

Item N3. Selection criteria, in the form of a selection function, or a justification for neglecting it, including whether n is fixed or random and the order of selection and measurement. Required.

Item N4. Dependence structure and any clustering step, including algorithm, settings, and how each is reproduced in the simulation. Required.

Item N5. Transformation and its justification. Required.

Item N6. Jittering scale and its reproduction in the simulation, where applicable. Required when applicable.

Item N7. Support rule, whether S1, S2, or S3, its details, and its reproduction in the simulation. Required.

Item N8. Null model, including density, parameters, provenance, and the bandwidth rule if a KDE. Required.

Item N9. Scan family, whether adjacent, pair, or fixed, and g_min as a ratio of scales together with whether it is in data or probability units, and P_max, and the boundary convention, and the empty-W convention, and the tie-breaking rule. Required.

Item N10. Gap-selection rule. Required.

Item N11. Nuisance-estimation method, the gap-exclusion loop, the truncated-likelihood treatment, the iteration count, and its reproduction in the simulation. Required.

Item N12. Statistic, with an explicit statement that it is one-sided. Required.

Item N13. N, the target tail probability and the Monte Carlo uncertainty. Required.

Item N14. Multiplicity procedure, including how multiple nulls are handled. Required.

Item N15. Lambda_obs together with its computation. Required.

Item N16. Empirical null distribution, including the point mass at zero and the fraction of empty-W datasets. Required.

Item N17. Exceedance probability together with the tail definition, whether >= or >. Required.

Item N18. Sensitivity analysis and the selection-effect analysis. Required.

Item N19. Code, data, seeds and software versions. Required.

Item N20. Statement that a gap is not described as physically significant solely because it is visually obvious, has a large score, was identified by a clustering algorithm, or has a small p-value under one null. Required.

Module 7: Required-When-Applicable Resolver
For each item that is required-when-applicable, checks whether the item is applicable to the submission. An item is applicable if the corresponding feature is present in the submission. Examples:

Item S11 (scale-separation record) is applicable if the STAG record contains a STAG-Discovered label.

Item S12 (partition sensitivity) is applicable if the STAG record contains a label above STAG-Unclassified.

Item S14 (threshold provenance) is applicable if the STAG record contains a threshold.

Item N2 (measurement uncertainty) is applicable if the pipeline specification declares measurement uncertainty.

Item N3 (selection criteria) is applicable if the pipeline specification declares selection effects.

Item N6 (jittering scale) is applicable if the pipeline specification declares jittering.

The resolver returns not_applicable for items that are not applicable.

Module 8: Cross-Check Engine
Compares the reported numbers in the manuscript with the numbers in the STAG annotations and the Null-Model Protocol specification.

For each number in the manuscript that matches a pattern for a reported number (for example, a p-value, a statistic, a ratio), the engine searches the annotations and specifications for a matching number. If a match is found and the numbers are equal within a tolerance, the item passes. If a match is found and the numbers differ, the item fails with the reason. If no match is found, the item is marked as code-dependent.

The engine reports the cross-check results.

Module 9: Checklist Report Builder
Assembles the checklist report. For each item in the STAG reporting standard and the Null-Model Protocol reporting standard, the status: pass, fail, missing, or not_applicable. Includes the reason for each fail or missing item.

Module 10: Summary Reporter
Produces the summary report. The number of items passed, the number of items failed, the number of items missing, and the number of items not applicable.

Module 11: Reproducibility Recorder
Records the STAG schema version, Null-Model Protocol schema version, checker version, submission identifier, and timestamp.

Module 12: Web Service Layer
Wraps the checker in an HTTP endpoint. Accepts a POST with the manuscript, the STAG annotation files, the Null-Model Protocol files, and the options. Returns the JSON report. Uses HTTP status 200 for a complete check, 422 for a check with failures, 400 for malformed input.

Module 13: Command-Line Interface
Parses arguments. Invokes Modules 1 through 11. Prints the report to stdout.

Full Reporting Standard Specification
6.1 STAG Reporting Standard
The STAG reporting standard is defined in Artifact 1, Section 14.1. It has 24 items. Each item is checked against the STAG annotation files and the manuscript text.

For each item, the checker:

Searches the STAG annotation files for the field or fields corresponding to the item.

Searches the manuscript text for a reference to the item (for example, a sentence that reports the node tuple rule, or a table that reports the agreement statistics).

Records the status: pass if the field is present in the annotation and referenced in the manuscript; fail if the field is present but incorrect or inconsistent with the manuscript; missing if the field is absent; not_applicable if the item is required-when-applicable and not applicable.

6.2 Null-Model Protocol Reporting Standard
The Null-Model Protocol reporting standard is defined in Section 12 of the Null-Model Protocol paper. It has 20 items. Each item is checked against the pipeline specification, the statistical gap results, and the manuscript text.

For each item, the checker:

Searches the pipeline specification and the statistical gap results for the field or fields corresponding to the item.

Searches the manuscript text for a reference to the item.

Records the status: pass, fail, missing, or not_applicable.

6.3 Cross-Check
The cross-check engine compares the reported numbers in the manuscript with the numbers in the annotations and specifications. The following numbers are cross-checked:

The observed statistic Lambda_obs.

The exceedance probability p.

The Monte Carlo uncertainty.

The empty-W fraction.

The point mass at zero.

The critical values for the scan families.

The power values for the scan families.

The scale-separation ratio.

The expansion parameter.

The band minimum ratio.

The agreement statistics (Krippendorff's alpha, Gwet's AC1).

The material-change result.

The node identity decision.

The entailment basis.

The intervention classification.

The cross-domain counterfactual dependence.

The derived display label.

The evidential anchoring status.

For each number, the engine searches the manuscript for a matching number. If a match is found and the numbers are equal within a tolerance, the item passes. If a match is found and the numbers differ, the item fails with the reason. If no match is found, the item is marked as code-dependent.

6.4 Required-When-Applicable Logic
The required-when-applicable logic is applied to the items that are marked as required-when-applicable. For each such item, the checker determines whether the corresponding feature is present in the submission. If the feature is present, the item is required and is checked. If the feature is absent, the item is not_applicable and is not checked.

The features that trigger required-when-applicable status are:

For the STAG reporting standard: the presence of a STAG-Discovered label, a label above STAG-Unclassified, a threshold, a characteristic scale, a reversibility attribute, a status dimension, a statistical gap interface, an uncertainty and alternatives section, and a scale-separation record.

For the Null-Model Protocol reporting standard: the presence of measurement uncertainty, selection effects, dependence structure, jittering, a KDE null, a pair scan family, a fixed scan family, a nuisance-estimation procedure, a multiplicity procedure, and a sensitivity analysis.

6.5 Detection Logic
The detection engine detects whether the manuscript uses STAG annotations, Null-Model Protocol specifications, or both. The detection is based on:

The presence of STAG annotation files in the submission.

The presence of Null-Model Protocol files in the submission.

The presence of STAG-specific terms in the manuscript text.

The presence of Null-Model Protocol-specific terms in the manuscript text.

The detection report records the detection result and the evidence. The possible results are: stag_only, null_model_only, both, neither.

6.6 Checklist Report Structure
The checklist report has the following structure:

submission_id (string)

detection (object: result, evidence)

stag_checklist (array of item objects)

null_model_checklist (array of item objects)

cross_check (array of cross-check objects)

summary (object: pass_count, fail_count, missing_count, not_applicable_count)

reproducibility (object: stag_schema_version, null_model_schema_version, checker_version, timestamp)

Each item object:

item_id (string, e.g., "S1" or "N1")

item_name (string)

status (pass, fail, missing, not_applicable)

reason (string or null)

evidence (string or null)

Each cross-check object:

field (string)

manuscript_value (number or string)

annotation_value (number or string)

status (match, mismatch, code_dependent)

reason (string or null)

Core Logic as Executable Pseudocode
7.1 Main Flow
function checkSubmission(manuscript, stagAnnotations, pipelineSpec, gapResults, options):

Step 1. manuscriptText = loadManuscript(manuscript, options).

Step 2. stagRecords = [].

if stagAnnotations is not null:

for annotation in stagAnnotations:

result = validateSTAGRecord(annotation, options.stagSchemaVersion).

if result.valid: stagRecords.push(annotation).

else: return {error: "invalid STAG record", details: result.errors}.

Step 3. nullModelSpec = null.

if pipelineSpec is not null: result = validatePipelineSpec(pipelineSpec, options.nullModelSchemaVersion); if result.valid: nullModelSpec = pipelineSpec; else: return {error: "invalid pipeline specification", details: result.errors}.

Step 4. gapResultsList = [].

if gapResults is not null:

for result in gapResults: validated = validateGapResult(result, options.nullModelSchemaVersion); if validated.valid: gapResultsList.push(result).

Step 5. detection = detectUsage(manuscriptText, stagRecords, nullModelSpec).

Step 6. stagChecklist = checkSTAGStandard(stagRecords, manuscriptText, detection).

Step 7. nullModelChecklist = checkNullModelStandard(nullModelSpec, gapResultsList, manuscriptText, detection).

Step 8. crossCheck = crossCheckNumbers(manuscriptText, stagRecords, nullModelSpec, gapResultsList).

Step 9. summary = buildSummary(stagChecklist, nullModelChecklist).

Step 10. report = buildReport(stagChecklist, nullModelChecklist, crossCheck, summary, detection, options).

Step 11. reproducibility = buildReproducibilityRecord(options, submissionId, timestamp).

Step 12. return {report, reproducibility}.

7.2 Detect Usage
function detectUsage(manuscriptText, stagRecords, nullModelSpec):

evidence = [].

if stagRecords.length > 0: evidence.push({type: "stag_files", count: stagRecords.length}).

if nullModelSpec is not null: evidence.push({type: "null_model_file"}).

stagTerms = ["STAG-Discovered", "evidential anchoring", "node tuple", "scale-separation record", "STAG-Definitional", "STAG-Unclassified"].

for term in stagTerms:

if manuscriptText.contains(term): evidence.push({type: "stag_term", term: term}).

nullModelTerms = ["empirical null distribution", "exceedance probability", "scan statistic", "empty-W fraction", "Monte Carlo uncertainty", "full-pipeline simulation"].

for term in nullModelTerms:

if manuscriptText.contains(term): evidence.push({type: "null_model_term", term: term}).

hasStag = evidence.some(e => e.type == "stag_files" or e.type == "stag_term").

hasNullModel = evidence.some(e => e.type == "null_model_file" or e.type == "null_model_term").

if hasStag and hasNullModel: return {result: "both", evidence: evidence}.

else if hasStag: return {result: "stag_only", evidence: evidence}.

else if hasNullModel: return {result: "null_model_only", evidence: evidence}.

else: return {result: "neither", evidence: evidence}.

7.3 Check STAG Standard
function checkSTAGStandard(stagRecords, manuscriptText, detection):

checklist = [].

for item in STAG_STANDARD_ITEMS:

status = checkSTAGItem(item, stagRecords, manuscriptText).

checklist.push({item_id: item.id, item_name: item.name, status: status.status, reason: status.reason, evidence: status.evidence}).

return checklist.

function checkSTAGItem(item, stagRecords, manuscriptText):

if item.required_when_applicable:

if not isApplicable(item, stagRecords): return {status: "not_applicable"}.

if stagRecords.length == 0: return {status: "missing", reason: "no STAG annotation files provided"}.

for record in stagRecords:

if hasField(record, item.field): found = true.

if not found: return {status: "missing", reason: "field " + item.field + " not found in STAG annotation"}.

if not manuscriptText.contains(item.name) and not manuscriptText.contains(item.term): return {status: "missing", reason: "item not referenced in manuscript"}.

return {status: "pass"}.

7.4 Check Null-Model Standard
function checkNullModelStandard(nullModelSpec, gapResults, manuscriptText, detection):

checklist = [].

for item in NULL_MODEL_STANDARD_ITEMS:

status = checkNullModelItem(item, nullModelSpec, gapResults, manuscriptText).

checklist.push({item_id: item.id, item_name: item.name, status: status.status, reason: status.reason, evidence: status.evidence}).

return checklist.

function checkNullModelItem(item, nullModelSpec, gapResults, manuscriptText):

if item.required_when_applicable:

if not isApplicableNullModel(item, nullModelSpec): return {status: "not_applicable"}.

if nullModelSpec is null: return {status: "missing", reason: "no pipeline specification provided"}.

if not hasField(nullModelSpec, item.field) and not hasField(gapResults[0], item.field): return {status: "missing", reason: "field " + item.field + " not found"}.

if not manuscriptText.contains(item.name) and not manuscriptText.contains(item.term): return {status: "missing", reason: "item not referenced in manuscript"}.

return {status: "pass"}.

7.5 Cross Check Numbers
function crossCheckNumbers(manuscriptText, stagRecords, nullModelSpec, gapResults):

crossChecks = [].

numbers = extractNumbers(manuscriptText).

for number in numbers:

match = findMatch(number, stagRecords, nullModelSpec, gapResults).

if match is not null:

if abs(number.value - match.value) <= tolerance: crossChecks.push({field: match.field, manuscript_value: number.value, annotation_value: match.value, status: "match"}).

else: crossChecks.push({field: match.field, manuscript_value: number.value, annotation_value: match.value, status: "mismatch", reason: "values differ"}).

else: crossChecks.push({field: "unknown", manuscript_value: number.value, annotation_value: null, status: "code_dependent"}).

return crossChecks.

7.6 Build Summary
function buildSummary(stagChecklist, nullModelChecklist):

allItems = stagChecklist.concat(nullModelChecklist).

passCount = count(item in allItems where item.status == "pass").

failCount = count(item in allItems where item.status == "fail").

missingCount = count(item in allItems where item.status == "missing").

notApplicableCount = count(item in allItems where item.status == "not_applicable").

return {pass_count: passCount, fail_count: failCount, missing_count: missingCount, not_applicable_count: notApplicableCount}.

7.7 Build Report
function buildReport(stagChecklist, nullModelChecklist, crossCheck, summary, detection, options):

return {submission_id: options.submissionId, detection: detection, stag_checklist: stagChecklist, null_model_checklist: nullModelChecklist, cross_check: crossCheck, summary: summary, reproducibility: {stag_schema_version: options.stagSchemaVersion, null_model_schema_version: options.nullModelSchemaVersion, checker_version: options.checkerVersion, timestamp: now()}}.

